Subject: [PATCH 57/68] The camera follows the app window's rotation, not the
 physical display's: sync CameraXFrameSource (F19, task 23)

F19, found live on the phone (APK 3, 2026-10-28): with the display at 90
(a landscape video in picture-in-picture) and the app portrait under a
fixed-rotation transform, three sessions logged "analysis 640x480 rotation
0 -> frames 640x480": the faces arrived sideways (NO_FACE, POOR_QUALITY,
CHALLENGE_FAILED; light and frame rate were fine). After an app restart,
"rotation 270 -> frames 480x640" and PASSED in 2.1 s. CameraX's default
target rotation is the physical display's, read at each bind.

- CameraXFrameSource from Mosip26 56bf83b (Part 1): the analysis and the
  preview are bound at the window's rotation (Activity.getDisplay() on API
  30+, which includes the fixed-rotation adjustments), logged as "target
  rotation <window> (display <physical>)"; a DisplayListener re-applies it
  after each display change until stop(), which unregisters it. The source
  takes the window from its lifecycle owner, the Activity, so the session,
  the benchmark capture and the still capture all get it.
- MainActivity.onConfigurationChanged (configChanges keeps the Activity)
  calls LivenessApi.onConfigurationChanged, which re-applies the window's
  rotation to the running session's camera: the window's new rotation may
  arrive after the display's change.
- engine-sync.txt: the engine commit (56bf83b) and the camera source's
  SHA-256. The four jars are unchanged: synced with -SkipBuild, then the
  branch's jars kept, as nothing else in the engine changed.
- CameraXFrameSourceRotationTest (Robolectric, 6): the bind uses the
  window's rotation, not the display's nor CameraX's default; a display
  change and refreshTargetRotation() re-apply it to the analysis and the
  preview; analysis only without a preview; stop() unregisters once; a bind
  after stop() never watches; an Activity's rotation and its
  DisplayListener. With the fix removed, three of them fail.

:liveness:testDebugUnitTest: 121 passing (115 + 6).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
